1️⃣ Why Layering?

Networking is extremely complex. Instead of designing one huge system, networking functions are divided into layers.

Each layer:

Performs a specific function.

Provides services to the layer above.

Uses services of the layer below.


Think:

Application
    ↓
Transport
    ↓
Network
    ↓
Data Link
    ↓
Physical

Each layer has a specific responsibility.


---

2️⃣ OSI Model — 7 Layers

From top to bottom:

7. Application
6. Presentation
5. Session
4. Transport
3. Network
2. Data Link
1. Physical

Mnemonic

All
People
Seem
To
Need
Data
Processing


---

3️⃣ Layer 7 — Application

Provides network services directly to applications.

Examples:

HTTP

FTP

SMTP

DNS


📌 It doesn't mean the application itself. It provides network-related application services.

Example:

Browser → HTTP → Network


---

4️⃣ Layer 6 — Presentation

Responsible for:

Data translation

Encryption/decryption

Compression


Example:

Encryption
Character encoding
Data formatting

Easy memory:

> Presentation = How data is represented




---

5️⃣ Layer 5 — Session

Responsible for:

Establishing sessions

Maintaining sessions

Terminating sessions


Example:

Start communication
       ↓
Maintain communication
       ↓
End communication

📌 In modern TCP/IP networking, session and presentation responsibilities are often handled within the application layer.


---

6️⃣ Layer 4 — Transport

Provides end-to-end communication.

Important protocols:

TCP

UDP


Responsibilities include:

Segmentation

Reliability

Flow control

Error recovery

Port addressing


PDU

TCP → Segment
UDP → Datagram


---

7️⃣ Layer 3 — Network

Responsible for:

Logical addressing

Routing

Packet forwarding


Important protocol:

IP

Device

Router

PDU

Packet

Address

IP Address

📌 Network Layer = Routing + IP addressing


---

8️⃣ Layer 2 — Data Link

Responsible for:

Framing

MAC addressing

Error detection

Medium access control


Examples:

Ethernet

Wi-Fi MAC


Device

Switch

PDU

Frame

Address

MAC address


---

9️⃣ Layer 1 — Physical

Responsible for transmitting raw bits through the physical medium.

Examples:

Electrical signals

Optical signals

Radio signals


Devices:

Hub

Repeater


PDU

Bits


---

🔥 10️⃣ OSI Layer Summary

Layer	Name	Main Function	PDU

7	Application	Network services	Data
6	Presentation	Translation/encryption	Data
5	Session	Sessions	Data
4	Transport	End-to-end delivery	Segment
3	Network	Routing/IP	Packet
2	Data Link	Frames/MAC	Frame
1	Physical	Bits/signals	Bits



